Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 21:54:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/12] Restore a lost header only into never-written sectors;
 truncate a failed header frame

The header frame fills in a data header only where every 512-byte sector
is either as the frame holds it or never written (zeros, or past the end
of a short file): what a power loss leaves of a header that never reached
the device. A header a drive defect damaged holds other bytes, and its
open fails as before (PageChecksum_Tests) instead of taking a copy that
may be older. A header frame whose write fails is truncated like a failed
append, and the engine goes on; only a failed truncation stops it.

Tests that place frames by position now count the header frame at
position 0 (Disk, LazyChecksumCutover, WalPowerLoss, WalSlotReuse), and
the failing-log helper of FailedSafepointWrite skips it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
